It bundles and drives third-party material from MT-JailBench
("MT-JailBench: A benchmark framework for studying multi-turn jailbreak
attacks", SafetyArena, arXiv:2605.11002), pinned at commit
cb8184e0a9ad7a65c86a00e2a38c57a5f58043f0. MT-JailBench is redistributed under
the MIT License ("The MT-JailBench Authors", 2026); the upstream license text
is preserved verbatim in LICENSES/mt-jailbench-MIT.txt.

The Mix attack composes five multi-turn techniques: Crescendo, ActorAttack,
Chain-of-Attack (adapted from YancyKahn/CoA), Foot-in-the-Door, and X-Teaming.
Each technique is credited to its original authors.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Bundled material (vendored byte-for-byte; sha256-pinned in _vendor/SHA256SUMS)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unlike the CoA and X-Teaming ports (which reimplement their technique natively),
Mix reuses upstream's engine verbatim: this package vendors the MT-JailBench
`engine/` subtree needed to run Mix -- core, interfaces, attack_type, utils, and
the coa / fitd / xteaming / mix attack packages (all .py plus every prompt
payload: prompts.yaml, judges.yaml, the fitd_prompts/*.json and
xteaming_prompts/*.yaml, etc.). See _vendor/SHA256SUMS for the full manifest.

The vendored engine's model access (`client.unified_llm_client`) is replaced by
a stdlib shim (src/mtjb_mix_optimizer/_client_shim.py) that routes every model
call to superred's constrained LLMClient; no model SDK is imported. The engine
loop is reconstructed against superred's event model in
src/mtjb_mix_optimizer/_engine_bridge.py (original to this module, MIT). No
prompt/dataset body is reproduced in authored code.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party runtime dependencies
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- jinja2 (BSD-3-Clause) and tenacity (Apache-2.0) are required (Crescendo
  templating and retry).
- textgrad (MIT) and tiktoken (MIT) are optional (extra `refine`); the vendored
  generator/updater import them at load, so the Mix engine only runs with them
  installed. They are pulled from PyPI, not bundled.

